97735595703125, 0.0010976791381835938, 0.006435394287109375, 0.006511688232421875, 0.0528564453125, 0.041595458984375, 0.0333251953125, 0.0202178955078125, 0.015167236328125, -0.01422882080078125, -0.035858154296875, 0.0114288330078125, 0.006988525390625, 0.00665283203125, -0.032989501953125, 0.002643585205078125, 0.0282745361328125, -0.0075531005859375, -0.0309295654296875, -0.0015573501586914062, -0.0071563720703125, 0.01491546630859375, -0.0069427490234375, -0.01898193359375, -0.0166015625, -0.01654052734375, 0.01611328125, -0.0197296142578125, 0.03302001953125, -0.0205841064453125, 0.0306396484375, -0.00286102294921875, -0.045440673828125, -0.0295562744140625, -0.00457000732421875, 0.018646240234375, -0.0269927978515625, -0.002826690673828125, 0.036651611328125, 0.0005612373352050781, 0.0015430450439453125, -0.0164031982421875, -0.0034084320068359375, 0.030975341796875, -0.0032672882080078125, 0.0313720703125, -0.002445220947265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits. GA provides a unified mathematical framework that can represent geometric transformations and relationships more naturally than traditional vector algebra, offering potential advantages in the analysis and synthesis of complex systems.\n\nA crucial aspect in this area is the transformation of the ordinary differential equations governing system behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, which facilitates analysis and control design. However, to fully leverage GA in this context, a definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of GA is required.\n\nPrevious works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hyper-complex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras (see, for example, [1], [2]), but a comprehensive definition within GA has not been fully established. In this work, we build upon these earlier efforts by introducing a definition of the Laplace transform specifically tailored for Geometric Algebra. Our approach is applicable to geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where the sum p + q \u2264 5; here, \"signature\" refers to the number of basis vectors with positive (p) and negative (q) squares in the algebra, which determines its structure and dimensionality.\n\nWe also discuss key properties of the proposed Laplace transform in GA, such as linearity and the handling of multivector-valued functions, and outline its potential applications in the analysis of electrical circuits and other dynamical systems. This extension aims to provide a more powerful and flexible toolset for engineers and researchers working with complex systems in the GA framework.\n\nReferences:\n[1] S. L. Adler, \"Quaternionic Quantum Mechanics and Quantum Fields,\" Oxford University Press, 1995.\n[2] D. Hestenes and G. Sobczyk, \"Clifford Algebra to Geometric Calculus: A Unified Language for Mathematics and Physics,\" Reidel, 1984."}}